# Media playback and output selection

Help someone see what is playing, choose a command the player supports and know which speaker or screen it will change. Artwork helps people recognise an item. It must not push out the track title, the playback state or the name of the player. Opening a card, navigating or changing a theme must never start playback or move audio to another device.

These examples are local previews with invented household values. They do not control a home. Open an example to see it at full width or read its HTML.

## Scope and native ownership

The native `media_player` domain supplies the playback states and the commands each integration supports. The media browser lists only players that can browse media. This page covers how to present those states and confirm a change. It does not add a media adapter.

## States and content decisions

Use the native state labels. The stored states are `off`, `on`, `idle`, `playing`, `paused`, `buffering`, `unavailable` and `unknown`.

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Playing or Paused | The item and the player. Offer Pause or Resume only when the player supports it. | Infer Paused from a volume of zero |
| Idle, Off or On | Each as its own state. Idle is on and accepting commands but not playing. On means no more detail is known. | Call every state that is not playing “stopped” |
| Buffering | The native label Buffering, with no advancing position | Show an invented progress percentage |
| Unknown or Unavailable | The missing state beside the player name | Mix retained artwork with current playback |
| No duration supplied | “Duration not supplied” | Draw a seek range that cannot be valid |

## Integration contract

1. Read the supported commands, source list, volume limits, duration and position from the integration. Choosing a source asks for a change. It is not ordinary navigation.
2. Keep the selected player apart from its input source. Do not assume grouping, casting or transfer of playback just because several players exist.
3. Use the native service path and its permissions. Show a command as requested until Home Assistant reports the new state. If a request is rejected, keep the previous reported state.
4. Estimate elapsed time only where the native model supports it. Stop estimating on pause, source change or lost connection. Never save an estimate as a new reading.

## Accessible interaction

- Name the play, pause, mute and source controls, and include the player name where it helps. Do not rely on a changing icon alone.
- Give volume and seek a name, limits, keyboard adjustment and value text such as “35%” or “2 minutes 10 seconds of 5 minutes”. Keep the native keyboard behaviour. [Continuous controls and alternatives](continuous-controls.html) owns the slider rules.
- Use empty alternative text for artwork when the title and artist next to it already say the same thing. Respect provider and album artwork rights. Avoid flashing visualisers and respect reduced motion.

## Failure and recovery

- If a source change is rejected, keep the selected source, say it was rejected and do not retry playback automatically.
- If there is no position, keep the item and its state and leave out the seek control. A missing artwork image must not hide the playback controls.
- If the connection drops during a request, say the result is not confirmed. After it reconnects, read the reported state before offering another command. See [Connection loss and recovery](connection-and-recovery.html).

